\details{
\strong{Sinks, sources and the unknown source.} A \emph{sink} is a community whose
origins are in question (say an infant's gut, or a hospital surface);
the \emph{sources} are samples of the environments it may have come from
(the mother's skin, vagina and stool; patients' skin; tap water). Each
environment's samples are pooled, and each read of the sink is assigned,
by the Gibbs sampler, to one of the environments or to an \emph{unknown}
source, whose composition is learned from the reads assigned to it. The
proportions are the shares of the sink's reads assigned to each, averaged
over the draws.

The model assumes that a sink is a mixture of its sources (and the
unknown). It is not when communities change after they arrive (growth,
competition, a different habitat), which can make sources look more or
less important than they were (Shenhav et al. 2019). In their simulations,
SourceTracker also tended to underestimate the unknown source.

\strong{The pipeline}, as SourceTracker2's:
\enumerate{
\item Each environment's samples are pooled by their \strong{mean}, truncated to
whole numbers (as SourceTracker2 does, although its README says
"rounded").
\item The pooled environments are rarefied to \code{depth}, and the sinks to
\code{sink_depth}, without replacement. A source environment or sink with
fewer reads than its depth is an error. With \code{loo = TRUE}, the
held-out environment is already rarefied, and \code{sink_depth} is unused.
\item For each sink, \code{n_restarts} chains each start from a uniform random
assignment of the reads, run \code{n_burnin + (n_draws - 1) * thin + 1}
passes over the reads (each in a new random order), and take a draw
(the reads' current assignment) after pass \code{n_burnin + 1} and every
\code{thin} passes after that.
}

A read of feature \eqn{t} is assigned to known source \eqn{v} with
weight \eqn{(m_{vt} + \alpha_1)/(m_v + \tau\alpha_1)\,(c_v + \beta)},
and to the unknown source with weight \eqn{(u_t + \alpha_2 n)/(U +
\alpha_2 n\tau)\,(c_U + \beta)}, where \eqn{m} are the rarefied source
counts, \eqn{c} the sink's reads currently assigned to each source,
\eqn{u} (sum \eqn{U}) those assigned to the unknown by feature, \eqn{n}
the sink's depth, and \eqn{\tau} the number of features: \strong{every column
of \code{counts}}, including features that are zero everywhere, as in
SourceTracker2. Removing empty features therefore changes the result a
little.

\strong{Rarefaction depth matters.} Rarefying the sources to \code{depth} sets how
much evidence each environment's feature distribution carries; rarefying
the sinks to \code{sink_depth} sets how many reads are assigned, and so how
smooth the unknown source is (\code{alpha2} is per read of the sink). Results
at different depths aren't directly comparable, and a deeper \code{depth}
makes rare features of the sources count.

\strong{Reproducibility.} Rarefying sink \eqn{i} uses the same random stream
as \code{\link[=rarefy]{rarefy()}} would for sample \eqn{i} of \code{counts}, so that each sink
is rarefied exactly as \code{rarefy(counts, sink_depth, seed = seed)}
rarefies it. Each chain has its own stream, chosen by the sink's
position in \code{counts} and the restart. The result is therefore the same
for any \code{cpus}, and a sink's result doesn't change when sinks after it
in \code{counts} are added or removed.

\strong{The stacked bar chart.} \code{plot(x)} draws one bar per sink, divided by
source, with the unknown in grey; \code{barplot(t(x), legend.text = TRUE)}
draws much the same with base graphics alone.
